          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What does \"code first\" mean, and what actually goes wrong if the two are sequenced the other way round?",
          "answer": "\"Code first\" is a sequencing instruction in the ICD-10-CM classification, the US clinical coding system. It means an underlying condition is reported before its manifestation (the way it shows up in the body). The paired instruction \"use additional code\" tells the coder to add the second code after the first. (Source: *ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection*.)\n\nHeart failure gives an example. Where heart disease is documented as due to hypertension, the hypertensive heart disease combination code is reported first, and a heart failure code is added to identify the type of heart failure. (Source: *Coding heart failure: I50, its mechanism axis and its acuity axis*.)\n\nThe retrieved passages do not say what goes wrong if the two are sequenced the other way round. They do not describe the effects of reversed sequencing, such as a claim being rejected or a record being misread. I can't fill that gap from memory, so that part of your question is unanswered here. A medical coder or the official ICD-10-CM guidelines would be the place to check it.\n\nThis is general coding information, not advice about any health condition. If you have a specific diagnosis or bill in mind, please raise it with the clinician or billing office involved.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
